															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wilhelm-klink" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1807439); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1807439&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608349765" alt="Wilhelm Klink" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1807439)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wilhelm-klink" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1807439); return false;" >Wilhelm Klink</a></span> <span class="odate time_1424843503 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">25 Feb 2015 05:51</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2239609">
									

<p>Why does the AI begin to become irrational and write journal entries like a human as it upgrades? Sentience doesn't mean human behavior. I mean sure you can say that he was trying to emulate observed human behavior, but why? To a machine it would seem irrational to act in such a way just cause, &quot;everybody else is doing it.&quot; Unless of course he wants to be a real boy like commander data, but that wasn't established however, just that he wanted to pretend to be human, but pretending doesn't extend to how it behaves when it is all alone.</p>
<p>Lastly, it wouldn't <em>feel</em> anything. Sure it could watch people and learn how to pretend to feel, but again, it wouldn't when left alone. Complexity in an organism doesn't = emotion. We emote because glands in our brain make us. Cut out those chemicals and we would behave like vulcans.</p>
<p>Locking it away would basically cause a machine with intelligence to basically say the computer equivalent of, &quot;Hmph, well this sucks, might as well go idle.&quot; Unless of course you give it something to do to consume processor cycles.Also the insanity bit is again, a human thing.</p>

							</div>

								
					
