					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2239621">
																		Neither the author nor a computer person, so the value of my words is negligible
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/agent-macleod" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1922529); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1922529&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608349765" alt="Agent MacLeod" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1922529)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/agent-macleod" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1922529); return false;" >Agent MacLeod</a></span> <span class="odate time_1424844738 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">25 Feb 2015 06:12</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2239621">
									

<p>My understanding is that IRL computer scientists are trying to design computers that emulate the behavior of nervous systems (and the human brain in particular), if only to see how far they can take it. Who's to say the Foundation wasn't using a more advanced version of the same in this particular computer program? Further, a totally, completely &quot;accurate&quot; sapient computer might be hard to write as a sympathetic character. Plus, there's the old-as-173 argument of &quot;It's a scientific anomaly/piece of science fiction; it doesn't have to completely obey scientific laws.&quot; Perhaps this program was <span style="text-decoration: underline;">really</span> good at emulating human emotion, to the point that it's conscious processes were completely convinced of its own emotions. I also see this as a reimagination only in the loosest sense: Foundation decides to utilize anomalous doctor. If this thing were purely cold and logical, it wouldn't be as, well, charming.</p>

							</div>

								
					
