																		Re: Neither the author nor a computer person, so the value of my words is negligible
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wilhelm-klink" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1807439); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1807439&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608349765" alt="Wilhelm Klink" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1807439)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wilhelm-klink" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1807439); return false;" >Wilhelm Klink</a></span> <span class="odate time_1424849163 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">25 Feb 2015 07:26</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2239646">
									

<p>Except you predicate all these explanations on something that you just made up separate from the story's contents. Also, even if you were a machine that was built to have a mind like a human's <strong>that doesn't mean you are going to act like one</strong>. You would have to be taught to behave like that and a machine wouldn't really experience emotion without the aforementioned glands; and this is apparently just a mainframe computer that accidentally became sentient.</p>
<p>In fact, let me really, really stress how important the glands are I keep mentioning. There is nothing like anything we experience out of emotion without them. There is no fear, no panic, no joy, sadness, anything without that chemical wizzing through the proper receptor. And a machine would find these to be irrational distractions that do nothing but impede its ability to think and make decisions.</p>
<p>Before you rebut to that, imagine if you never felt <em>anything</em> and you went through life making decisions based on what would be the most logical and productive based on the data available. Suddenly you find you can't clean your apartment because you don't <em>want</em> to anymore. Logically, the apartment needs to be cleaned and nothing is impeding you at all from doing it besides the sudden dislike of doing it. Wouldn't you believe this to be an issue to yourself? Would you think it is strange that you are suddenly taking odd risks to your well being, no longer gauging things based on whether it would actually work, just going on this, &quot;gut feeling&quot; you suddenly have for things despite it making no reasonable sense?</p>

							</div>

								
					
